drift-velocity-crossed-fields.interpretation:
The equation 
$$\mathbf{v}_d = \frac{\mathbf{E} \times \mathbf{B}}{B^2}$$
quantifies the drift velocity, 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{v}_d \end{vmatrix}
, of charged particles when they are simultaneously subjected to an electric field, 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{E} \end{vmatrix}
, and a magnetic field, 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{B} \end{vmatrix}
, which are perpendicular to each other. The electric field exerts a force 
\begin{vmatrix} q\mathbf{E} \end{vmatrix}
 on a particle with charge 
\begin{vmatrix} q \end{vmatrix}
, tending to accelerate it. The magnetic field exerts a Lorentz force 
\begin{vmatrix} q(\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) \end{vmatrix}
 on a particle moving with velocity 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{v} \end{vmatrix}
. In crossed fields, these forces combine.  The cross product 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{E} \times \mathbf{B} \end{vmatrix}
 indicates that the resulting drift velocity 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{v}_d \end{vmatrix}
 is perpendicular to both the electric and magnetic fields. The magnitude of the drift velocity is directly proportional to the magnitude of the electric field and inversely proportional to the magnitude of the magnetic field squared. This implies that a stronger magnetic field

drift-velocity-crossed-fields.symmetry_origin:
This formula can be derived from the Lorentz force law, 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{F} = q(\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) \end{vmatrix}
, by considering the steady-state condition where the net force on a charge carrier is balanced by resistive forces, or by analyzing the trajectory of a charged particle in crossed fields, leading to a net drift motion. It arises from the fundamental interactions of charged particles with electromagnetic fields.

drift-velocity-crossed-fields.limits_and_boundary:
As the electric field 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{E} \end{vmatrix}
 approaches zero, the drift velocity 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{v}_d \end{vmatrix}
 also approaches zero, as there is no driving force for the directed motion. As the magnetic field magnitude 
\begin{vmatrix} B \end{vmatrix}
 approaches zero, the drift velocity would theoretically approach infinity if the formula were applied without considering other effects like collisions, which become dominant in the absence of a magnetic field to curve the trajectories.  If the magnetic field is not perpendicular to the electric field, the formula changes, and the drift velocity has components parallel and perpendicular to 
\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{B} \end{vmatrix}
.
